WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.680
"Please call me Max" were the first words Perutz offered almost every young

2
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.060
scientist who came to him.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.800
He had a model of hemoglobin hanging from his office ceiling and had been chasing

4
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.720
the one molecule since 1937 — twenty-two years before he solved it.

5
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:23.353
The breakthrough was a trick: — soak the crystals in mercury and silver to

6
00:00:23.353 --> 00:00:25.133
triangulate the missing information.

7
00:00:25.913 --> 00:00:27.833
Kendrew ran it first, on myoglobin.

8
00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:34.427
There was no computer screen in 1958, so the group drew the electron density by hand

9
00:00:34.427 --> 00:00:39.347
and stacked the contours on dozens of sheets of clear plastic until the molecule

10
00:00:39.347 --> 00:00:41.187
climbed up into three dimensions.

11
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:47.327
What rose out of the sheets was a fat, irregular rod doubling back on itself with no

12
00:00:47.327 --> 00:00:52.607
symmetry anyone had predicted the lab called it the sausage model, and Kendrew

13
00:00:52.607 --> 00:00:57.967
admitted only to surprise at its "unexpected twists," conceding the shape could not

14
00:00:57.967 --> 00:00:59.587
be recommended on aesthetic grounds.

15
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.530
The first protein ever seen was a mess.

16
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.210
It was also exactly right.

17
00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:07.130
The two men shared the 1962 Nobel Prize.
